"According to the passage, which organ is anger said to injure, and which organ is fear said to injure?": "8697cafec364",
"Alongside the six excesses, which attack from outside, Chinese medicine describes seven emotions capable of causing illness from inside: anger, joy, worry, sadness, grief, fear, and fright. The claim isn't that having emotions is dangerous — feeling angry or afraid occasionally is a normal, healthy response to life. The claim is that any one of these seven, when sustained at an extreme level over time, can injure a specific organ, the same way any of the six excesses can, just from the inside rather than the outside.": "95e65ba759ce",
"Alongside the six excesses, which attack from outside, Chinese medicine describes seven emotions capable of causing illness from inside: anger, joy, worry, sadness, grief, fear, and fright. The claim isn't that having emotions is dangerous — feeling angry or afraid occasionally is a normal, healthy response to life. The claim is that any one of these seven, when sustained at an extreme level over time, can injure a specific organ, the same way any of the six excesses can, just from the inside rather than the outside. Each emotion is matched to a zang organ from earlier in this course, following the same five-element logic. Anger is said to injure the liver. Excessive joy — meaning overstimulation, not ordinary happiness — is said to injure the heart. Worry and pensiveness are said to injure the spleen. Sadness and grief are said to injure the lung. Fear is said to injure the kidney. A person under prolonged anger developing digestive trouble, or a person under prolonged grief developing shortness of breath, is showing exactly the pattern this framework predicts. This is not a claim that emotions are the only cause of illness, nor that feeling a difficult emotion once will damage an organ. The seven emotions become a medical concern the same way the six excesses do: not through a single instance, but through a sustained, extreme pattern that the body isn't given enough time or support to recover from.": "ecad471f856c",
